#!markdown

# Quiver — a tour in a notebook

A plotting library where **the units are part of the type**. `t [s]` and `x [m]` on the axes below
were not typed by anyone; they were derived from the unit witnesses the data was tagged with, and
the compiler would have rejected the figure if the two series disagreed about what a metre is.

Everything in this notebook runs against the packages as published. Nothing is configured.

> **A note on the format.** This is a `.dib`, not an `.ipynb`: plain text, one cell per `#!`
> directive, no embedded output, and therefore reviewable in a pull request the same way the
> golden masters are. VS Code and `dotnet repl` open it directly. GitHub will not render it — the
> trade that buys a diff you can read.

#!fsharp

#r "nuget: Quiver.Core"
#r "nuget: Quiver.Units"
#r "nuget: Quiver.Backend.Svg"
#r "nuget: Quiver.Plot"
#r "nuget: Quiver.Interactive"

open Quiver

#!markdown

That is the whole setup. `Quiver.Interactive` ships an `extension.dib` the kernel runs on load, so
the formatters are already registered — `Quiver.Formatters.register ()` is available for a kernel
that does not run package extensions, and is not needed here.

## The headline

Free fall. The `x` array is `float<m>` because `0.5 * 9.81<m/s^2> * t * t` is, and the axis label
follows from that and from nothing else.

#!fsharp

let t = Array.init 24 (fun i -> float i * 0.4<s>)
let x = t |> Array.map (fun t -> 0.5 * 9.81<m/s^2> * t * t)

Chart.line U.second U.metre t x
|> Chart.withName "model"
|> Chart.withTitle "Free fall"
|> Chart.withSubtitle "displacement against elapsed time"
|> Chart.withLegend LegendTopLeft
|> Chart.withGrid true true

#!markdown

No `.Show()`, no `display`, no save-then-open. The cell's value *is* a `Figure`, and a `Figure`
knows how to be an SVG.

Add a measured series to the same panel and the legend, the domain union and the colour cycle all
follow:

#!fsharp

let measured = t.[..5] |> Array.map (fun t -> 0.5 * 9.6<m/s^2> * t * t)

Chart.line U.second U.metre t x
|> Chart.withName "model"
|> Chart.addScatter U.second U.metre t.[..5] measured
|> Chart.withName "measured"
|> Chart.withTitle "Free fall"
|> Chart.withLegend LegendTopLeft
|> Chart.withGrid true true

#!markdown

## What the type system is actually doing

Try to put displacement and temperature on the same axis. Both are `float`s at runtime; only one of
them is a length.

The formatter does not throw — a display formatter that throws replaces a good message with a
kernel error — so the reason is rendered where the figure would have been:

#!fsharp

let temperature = t |> Array.map (fun t -> 293.0<K> + 0.5<K/s> * t)

Chart.line U.second U.metre t x
|> Chart.withName "displacement"
|> Chart.addLine U.second U.kelvin t temperature
|> Chart.withName "temperature"

#!markdown

Note *which* error that is. It is not "cannot convert K to m" — the arrays are separately
well-typed. It is the one dimensional mistake a compiler cannot catch, caught at figure-compile
time with both layers named.

## The same figure, as TeX

`Theme.tikz` swaps the emitter's target and the unit spelling together: axis labels become
`siunitx` commands, so the units are typeset by the same package that typesets the rest of the
paper, in the document's own fonts.

#!fsharp

#r "nuget: Quiver.Backend.Tikz"

let figure =
    Chart.line U.second U.metre t x
    |> Chart.withName "model"
    |> Chart.withTitle "Free fall"
    |> Chart.withTheme Theme.tikz

Chart.render TikzBackend.render (Chart.size 320.0 200.0) figure
|> fun tex -> tex.Split('\n') |> Array.filter (fun l -> l.Contains "qty") |> String.concat "\n"

#!markdown

`\qty{}{\second}` and `\qty{}{\metre}`, from the unit witnesses, with nothing configured but the
theme. Paste the full output into a LaTeX document between `\begin{document}` and `\end{document}`
and it compiles.

## The colour system

§9 of the design asks for "a validator that checks contrast and CVD-safety, because a physics
library that renders fields in jet is embarrassing". Colours, ramps and palettes all display, so
the validator's answer is readable rather than a record to squint at.

#!fsharp

Palette.okabeIto

#!fsharp

Palette.validate Color.white Palette.okabeIto

#!markdown

Four vision models, the worst one in bold, and the two entries that were closest under it.

**It says `FAILS`, and that is the honest answer.** Okabe–Ito stays distinguishable under all three
dichromacies — every separation clears the floor — but two of its eight colours are very light, and
against a *white* background its yellow lands at 1.32:1, far below WCAG's 3:1 for graphical objects.
Quiver does not "fix" the palette, because a corrected Okabe–Ito is not Okabe–Ito. It reorders:
`Palette.series` demotes the yellow to index 6 and the black to index 7, so you would need seven
series before you met one, and the dark theme swaps the black for white outright.

That reordering is what makes a *theme* pass where the raw set does not:

#!fsharp

Palette.validate Theme.dark.PanelBackground Palette.seriesDark

#!markdown

`Ok`, weakest contrast 3.15:1 — the same eight hues with the black swapped out, on the background
they are actually drawn on. The validator's job is to tell you which of those two situations you
are in rather than to reassure you, and the test suite pins both.

The sequential ramps are perceptually ordered in Oklab, and the bars below are resampled *through*
Oklab rather than handed to the browser as stops — a CSS gradient interpolates in sRGB, which is
precisely the artefact these ramps exist to avoid.

#!fsharp

Palette.viridis

#!fsharp

Palette.diverging

#!markdown

Blue to yellow is where the difference is visible without measuring: the perceptual path goes
through a green, the sRGB one through a dead grey.

#!fsharp

Ramp.ofColors "blue-yellow" [| Palette.blue; Palette.yellow |]

#!markdown

## Below the front door

The three types the rest of the library is built out of also display, which is how you inspect a
figure that is not doing what you expected.

A physical dimension, in Core's fallback spelling and in the SI unit `Quiver.Units` derives from it:

#!fsharp

Dim.sub Dim.length (Dim.pow 2 Dim.time)

#!markdown

A unit witness — the runtime value that carries a measure's meaning. Degrees Celsius is the
interesting one: affine, so its conversion to SI is not a factor alone.

#!fsharp

NonSi.degreeCelsius

#!markdown

An erased scale: the axis exactly as a backend sees it, after the measure is gone and a `Dim` has
taken its place.

#!fsharp

Erase.scale (Scale.log10 U.metre (1.0<m>, 1000.0<m>))

#!markdown

And the IR itself. Nine cases, and a census so you can tell how much of it there is:

#!fsharp

Chart.toScene (Chart.size 480.0 300.0) figure

#!markdown

## Sizing

Figures compile at 720×450 in a notebook — narrower than `Chart.defaultSize`, because a cell is
narrower than 800 pixels. Change it for one cell, or for the session:

#!fsharp

Html.figureAt (Chart.size 1000.0 380.0) figure

#!fsharp

Formatters.registerWith (Chart.size 560.0 350.0)
figure
